# Diagrams: Always Use Mermaid

When adding or editing diagrams in markdown files, **always** use Mermaid syntax inside fenced code blocks (` ```mermaid `). Never use ASCII art, Unicode box-drawing characters, or plain-text diagrams.

## Choosing the Right Diagram Type

| Concept | Mermaid Type |
|---------|-------------|
| Architecture / data flow | `flowchart TB` or `flowchart LR` |
| Sequence of interactions | `sequenceDiagram` |
| Timeline / schedule | `gantt` |
| Component hierarchy | `flowchart TB` with `subgraph` |
| State machine | `stateDiagram-v2` |
| Class / entity relationships | `classDiagram` or `erDiagram` |

## Example

```markdown
` ``mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Service A] -->|REST| B[Service B]
    B --> C[(Database)]
` ``
```

## What NOT to Do

- No `+--` / `|` box borders
- No Unicode `┌─┐ │ └─┘` box-drawing
- No arrow characters like `-->`, `->`, `=>` in plain text
- No indented text trees pretending to be architecture diagrams

File tree listings (` ├── `, ` └── `) for showing directory structure are acceptable as plain code blocks since Mermaid has no equivalent.
